<h1>2024 Barometer: the employee experience in France — where things stand</h1>
<p class="meta">Spaces That Matter — Blog &amp; Resources</p>
<p class="lead">1,200 employees surveyed across 8 sectors, nationwide. Our first national barometer reveals a persistent gap between what companies invest in their spaces and what their teams actually experience.</p>
<p>In 2024, for the first time, Spaces That Matter conducted a national barometer on the perceived quality of workspaces in France. A rigorous methodology, a representative sample, questions structured around the 8 dimensions of our framework: here are the main takeaways.</p>

<h2>Methodology</h2>
<p>The study was conducted in September 2024 among 1,200 French employees working in an office (full-time or part-time), selected to be representative of the French working population by sector, company size, status (manager / non-manager), gender and region.</p>
<p>The 8 sectors represented: financial services, consulting &amp; audit, manufacturing, healthcare, public administration, tech &amp; digital, real estate &amp; construction, education &amp; research.</p>
<p>The questionnaire is based on the 8 dimensions of the STM framework, with 48 questions rated on a scale of 1 to 10.</p>

<h2>Finding 1: An average score of 5.8/10 — below the performance threshold</h2>
<p>The overall average score across the 8 dimensions is 5.8 out of 10. This score is below the STM certification threshold set by our scientific committee. In other words, the majority of French workspaces do not reach the level of human performance required to be certified.</p>
<p>This figure is all the more striking given that 2021-2024 saw massive corporate investment in office real estate — to adapt spaces to hybrid work, draw employees back to the office and improve post-pandemic working conditions.</p>
<p>The gap between the investment made and the experience perceived is one of the main takeaways of this barometer.</p>

<h2>Finding 2: The “Governance and participation” dimension — the weakest</h2>
<p>With an average score of 4.2/10, the “Governance and participation” dimension is the weakest of all. It measures the extent to which employees were consulted, involved and informed when decisions were made about their workspaces.</p>
<p>78% of respondents say they were never consulted before their office was redesigned. 62% say they don't know where to report a problem with their workspace. 54% say their feedback is not taken into account.</p>
<p>“One day we were told the office had changed. We came in on a Monday morning — it was done.” — Respondent, consulting sector</p>

<h2>Finding 3: Acoustics — the leading source of dissatisfaction</h2>
<p>Among the sub-dimensions of physical and sensory well-being, acoustics has the lowest score: 4.7/10 on average. 67% of respondents say they are regularly disturbed by noise during their working hours. 41% say noise prevents them from concentrating “often” or “very often”.</p>
<p>These results are consistent with existing research — but their magnitude in the French context is notable. France has widely adopted open-plan offices (73% of the employees surveyed work in a fully or mostly open space) without always investing in complementary acoustic solutions.</p>

<h2>Finding 4: Tech and finance — out in front, but not unscathed</h2>
<p>The tech and finance sectors have the best overall scores (6.8/10 and 6.4/10 respectively). These sectors were among the first to invest in “next-generation” offices — with budgets per square foot well above average.</p>
<p>But even in these sectors, blind spots remain. The “Meaning and identity” dimension — which measures the consistency between the space and the organization's culture — scores only 5.9/10 in the tech sector. A paradox for companies that often cultivate a strong corporate culture.</p>

<h2>Finding 5: Remote work as a revealer, not a solution</h2>
<p>63% of respondents say their office disappoints them and that this is one of the reasons they prefer to work from home. This figure calls for a nuanced look at the relationship between remote work and the quality of office spaces.</p>
<p>Remote work is not just an organizational or lifestyle preference. For a majority of employees, it is also a response to an office environment that does not meet their needs. Improving the office experience is therefore a lever for bringing people back — far more effective than mandatory attendance policies.</p>

<h2>Finding 6: The label as a signal of commitment</h2>
<p>Finally, an open question asked respondents what would encourage them to come to the office more often. Among the most frequent answers: “Having my feedback taken into account” (mentioned by 58%), “Spaces suited to the type of work I do” (52%), “Knowing that my employer measures and improves the space” (44%).</p>
<p>This last figure is particularly significant. The process of measuring and improving — regardless of the result — is valued in itself by employees. The STM label is seen as a strong signal of the employer's commitment to its teams.</p>

<h2>Conclusion and outlook</h2>
<p>This first national barometer paints a worrying but hopeful picture. French workspaces suffer from significant shortfalls in terms of human experience — but these shortfalls are identified, measurable, and therefore fixable.</p>
<p>Spaces That Matter is committed to repeating this barometer every year, to track changes in the quality of workspaces in France and document the impact of the improvement initiatives undertaken by pioneering organizations.</p>
<p>The next barometer will be published in October 2025. Organizations wishing to contribute to this initiative and have their own spaces assessed can contact us.</p>
